Subject: How to flush UBC buffers from Fortran 90?
Date: 1997/05/28
Message-ID: <A.Panjkov-ya02408000R2805971052350001@news.latrobe.edu.au>#1/1
X-Deja-AN: 244366425
Organization: La Trobe University School of Mathematics
Newsgroups: comp.unix.osf.osf1,comp.lang.fortran



Hi...

I'm running Fortran 90 under Digital Unix 3.2g.   Sometimes I like to have
my program print a little ticker on the screen to show me where it's up to:

stdout=6
write(stdout,'(1x,i10, a1)', advance='no') icount, achar(13)

The idea here is that the advance='no' option suppresses the newline
character.  The achar(13) takes us back to the start of the line, so the
number
printed for icount is overwritten each time:   a nice one line ticker. 
(Note that this is stock standard Fortran 90, with an easy-to-control
machine dependent bit, the use of achar(13).  This is the sort of thing I
used to do with the + carriage control character (and the fpr utility on
systems that do not follow Fortran carriage control rules.)

If you run this as is, all the output appears long after the program has
been past the icount stage.  Aha, I thought, buffering, read the manual.  I
was referred to flush(3f) and fsync(3f).  

Ok, let's try:

stdout=6
write(stdout,'(i10, a1)', advance='no') icount, achar(13)
call flush(stdout)

No joy.    It appears that flush(3f) doesn't quite spit everything onto the
screen.  I think that what is happening is that flush(3f) flushes the
program's internal buffers to the *kernel's* buffers.  Right, so let's try
this fsync(3f) thing.   Where's the man page? 

$ man 3f fsync
There is not an entry for fsync in section 3.

What?  The Fortran 90 User  manual says (5.5.7)  "To request that UBC
system buffers be written to disk, use the fsync library routine (see
fsync(3f) and Chapter 11)."


Just for fun, put in a call to (the incompletely documented) fsync(3f) anyway.

...
call fsync()

This compiles and links, but I still don't get instant output.


QUESTIONS:

1)  Why haven't I got the man page for fsync(3f) on my system?  Is it in
some Layered Software Product I haven't installed?

2)  Why are internal buffers set up inside fortran programs when the OS
kernel provides buffers as well?    

3)  I tried using the getfd function to try and get the file descriptor
associated with logical unit 6 (stdout).  It returned a -1 signalling an
error.  If I knew the file descriptor, I could call the C version of fsync 
(maybe).  How can I find the file descriptor?

4) Does fsync flush the terminal i/o buffers as well as the disk buffers?

5) What is the interaction between non-advancing output and terminal buffering?
Why doesn't a call to flush(3f) flush queued non-advancing output?  Can I
control buffering  (maybe switching off line buffering)?  stty raw doesn't
seem to help.

6)  I thought standard error was unbuffered?  This appears not to be the case.

7)  How does (and should) fpr interact with nonadvancing output?


I don't think advance='no' is purely for simple prompts, but unless I can
flush queued non-advancing output without sending an end-of-record at some
point, that seems to be all I can do with it.

Thanks for your help!

-- 
--Andrej
