Maximillien Lincourt wrote:
> 
> I'm trying to use the getopts argument parser in a sh shell script, on
> DEC's OSF1 (4.0 and on SGI 6.x) but I seem to be having some soft of
> problems using it. It seems that the getopts does not get called! is the
> man page wrong or there's something I don't understand, could it be that
> getopts path is wrong ?
> 
> There's also a problem with the shift  command, is the syntax correct
> with all the parentheses on the sh man page on SGI they use
> 
> shift `expr $OPTIND - 1`
> 

As far as I can make out, /bin/getopts is a total waste of space,
written by someone who doesn't know the first law of Unix programming:
"A Unix process cannot alter the environment of its parent".

getopts has to be a shell built-in command, otherwise it won't work. ksh
has it, bash has it and many implementations of sh have it.

Change the first line of your script to #!/bin/ksh
and you should begin to get consistent behaviour between SGI and DEC.

Any comments from DEC about this?

Bob
