>As far as I can make out, /bin/getopts is a total waste of space,
>written by someone who doesn't know the first law of Unix programming:
>"A Unix process cannot alter the environment of its parent".

Don't be so harsh.  I assume that DEC is attempting to conform to the
letter of POSIX.2, which requires that certain `regular' builtins
(of which `getopts' is one) ``be implemented in a manner so that
they can be accessed via the POSIX.1 exec family of functions.''

POSIX.2 doesn't say they have to be useful, but they must be available.
For your amusement, `read', `cd', `alias' and the job control builtins
(fg/bg/jobs) must also be present in the file system as executables.
