Subject: Re: Dereferencing NULL pointer?
Date: 1997/05/21
Message-ID: <x7n2po7afl.fsf@hexadecimal.uoregon.edu>#1/1
X-Deja-AN: 243560631
References: <5lteva$q0e@charnel.ecst.csuchico.edu>
Organization: University of Oregon, Eugene
Newsgroups: comp.unix.osf.osf1


tsaylor@ecst.csuchico.edu (Todd M. Saylor) writes:

   I am currently in the process of porting an application to Digital UNIX
   4.0b (it's never been run on an alpha before).  The app runs fine on HP/UX,
   AIX, DG/UX (motorola and Intel), AT&T/NCR.  One problem has come up on
   previous ports, that is the ability to dereference a NULL pointer.  For the
   OS's that hate this, we've always found a linker option to allow it
   (basically return a null character) - for example on DG it would be '-z
   lowzeros'.
   
   The following code demonstrates what I mean:
   
   main()
   {
   char *x;
   
   x = (char *)NULL;
   
   printf("Null string = '%s'\n", x); /* This works fine */
   printf("Null Char = '%c'\n", *x); /* This get a seg. violation */
   }
   
   Now this may be bad form, but I was wondering if anyone knows of a
   switch/option to allow it?
   
   TIA
   
   [Posted for Aaron Johnson / aaron@bi-tech.com]

Your example program is inherently wrong.  A NULL pointer in ANSI C is
(void *) 0 -- it's valid to cast 0 to any pointer type to get a NULL,
and it's also valid to cast void * to any other pointer type; while
"#define NULL ((void *) 0)" is commonly used, it is no longer necessary
to use NULL as an pointer constant when you can use 0 instead.  A ASCII
NUL character (note the different spelling) is '\0'.  You don't get a
NUL character by dereference of a NULL pointer, because dereference of a
NULL pointer is undefined -- some architectures allow it but could
return any value whatsoever, while others generate SIGSEGV if you do it,
so you should never depend on it being possible in a portable program.
You need to read a C book, and so did the authors of the program you are
porting.  In the long run you will be much better off fixing the program
to eliminate the bogus NULL references than you are trying to find a way
to make it allowable on the architectures you port the program to.  I've
been through such messes already and most such NULL reference problems
introduce many subtle bugs.

-- 
