[ Raymond N Shwake ]

>>  Yes, Digital supports "choice", but where is its current emphasis? How
>>  many development dollars go to each platform? Where is the marketing
>>  group spending its dollars? What is the porting precedence for Digital
>>  tools and applications? From what I can tell as an outsider, it appears
>>  that the installed base is still mostly VMS, current sales are led by
>>  Digital UNIX, but emphasis and promotion goes to Windows NT.

Not to mention a totally absurd pricing structure which favours NT and tends
to steer people away from Digital UNIX (and probably to other vendors).

Example, based on the latest information I have from our Digital salesfolks:
Consider an AlphaServer 2100, with four 233 MHz EV4 and 128 Mbyte of memory.
Buying it with Digital UNIX costs more than 1.5 times as much as getting the
same system with NT.  And when you're talking about systems costing several
tens of thousands of dollars, this is *not* a trivial amount of money.

If Digital really wants to be taken seriously as a provider of UNIX products,
they'd better reasses their pricing scheme.

And let's not forget the Personal Workstation.  When first announced, these
were an NT-only product.  And for quite some time, there was no indication
Digital would ever offer anything except NT on them.  That situation has of
course changed recently.  But, as other people have pointed out, it's clear
where Digital's emphasis is.

