"Stewart McKain" <smckain@ozemail.com.au> writes:
> Does anyone have any comments on 4mm DDS3 or DLT tape drives?
> I need to backup 16 - 20Gb of data.
> Do the 4mm DDS3 125m tapes stretch or have problems?
> Digital Linear Tape looks robust but the cost of the tapes make it hard to
> justify over DDS3.

We've been using DLT for about a year now.  First on our old DECstation
Ultrix box, and now on our new Alpha OSF box.  We've got the flavor that
holds 15 Gbytes uncompressed, about 30 Gig compressed (assuming the
cononical 2:1 compression ratio, Your Mileage May Vary, of course).  Other
drives hold up to 35 Gig uncompressed, and if you really want to spend money
on hardware, you can get one with an automatic tape changer.

We love it.  My backup strategy consists of running level-0 dumps on every
file system (a total of 20+ Gbytes) onto a single tape via a script in the
middle of the night.  It's essentially a string of "dump 0" commands to the
no-rewind tape device.  In the morning, we pop out the tape, and pop in a
new one, rotating through a stack of about 10.

I don't run DDS tapes on our Unix boxes (we do use them for our AppleShare
server), but another group here does use DDS.  They seem to have problems
with the higher capacity tapes.  I'd vote for the DLT as the way of the
future.
