(I've tried to put  relevant keywords in the subject header...
And I apologize for the length of the message, but sometimes people leave o=
ut the
crucial bits, and I'm trying to avoid this.)

We have a bunch of rackmount AlphaServer 1000a 5/300's, and a 2100a 5/300, =
pedestal model.

All machines have worked fine with their network cards running at 10Mbits; =
the problems have come
about at 100Mbits. There are 3 separate problems that I've encountered, whi=
ch stump me. The first
seems to be a hardware level issue; the 2nd and 3rd may be more software re=
lated, perhaps.

Problem 1. One of the 1000a's, with DE500-XA network cards in slots 12 and =
13 (primary PCI bus), ran
   for weeks fine on a 10Mbit segment. Moving to the computer room, and giv=
ing it a 100Mbit switched
   port on the hub, setting ewa_mode to fast, it will NOT get a link light =
on the card in slot 12. We've done the
   obvious things: switched with known good patches etc. The card tests OK,=
  (see question 1 below),
  and the other card gets link also.  Identically configured machines have =
no problem.  I'm a little reluctant
  to believe the card is bad, but maybe so.  See after the .signature for c=
onsole output.

  Question 1.1: Are there more tests that I can run from the console level =
of the cpu? =

                     (Digital's  install booklet talks about using EzWorks,=
 some PC based application, =

                     to test and configure the network cards. Does anyone k=
now how to run such tests on a =

                    real machine?? )

  Question 1.2:  Is there a general problem with the DE500-XA's?  It would =
appear that all the
                         DE500-AA improves is with the autosensing mode (us=
eful) and the autoboot ROM
                         support (useless), is there anything else?

  Question 1.3: We're initially doing half-duplex at 100Mbit, has anyone ex=
perience with full-duplex
                        mode?

Problem 2.  An identically configured machine to that in Problem 1. is also=
 connected to
                2 100Mbit networks. It gets link lights on power up; the ne=
twork tests turn the link off
               and on briefly. The operating system is Digital Unix 4.0b, a=
nd on boot up the link light on tu0
              goes OFF (see the uerf output below, note the "no link" on tu=
0 message). There is a little
              attempt at generating net  activity with ntpdate, then ypbind=
 runs, and by the time an NFS
              mount is attempted over this interface, the link comes on, an=
d the rest is all normal from
              there on.  Note that tu1's link light never died. =

             So this machine is functional but I'm not happy about this.

            I've gotten both the huge Feb and April 4.0b patches, neither d=
iscuss this issue.

      Question 2.1: Is this a real problem, i.e., is it a bug in DU4.0b, an=
d will it cause
              any production problems? (That is, will the machine arbitrari=
ly change link states...)

Problem 3. The 2100a we have functioned excellently for several months runn=
ing DU3.2g, with a
            single DE435 10Mbit ethernet card. I got the upgrade fever; and=
 the machine was
            reconfigured to run 4.0b, with a firmware upgrade to 4.7 done, =
new SCSI controller
             cards, and 2 DE500-XA's installed. See the console output for =
Problem 3 section
            for firmware details, and where the cards are installed. There =
is no link light problem
            on powerup, both cards see their 100Mbit networks. The same pro=
blem as in Problem 2
            happens with the primary interface tu0 (link off during boot; c=
omes back on after
           some attempts to generate network activity during boot). However=
 this is only the
           beginning. =

           The real problem is that when one logs into the machine over tu0=
, and does anything
           that generates network activity (e.g., cd /usr; ls -lR with outp=
ut to an rsh session), after
          about thirty lines of output or less, the machine appears to free=
ze, losing all network
          activity for about a minute or more, and this message appears on =
the console:

            tu0: transmit FIFO underflow: threshold raised to: 256 bytes

=A0          After this life resumes; but unpredictively later, one gets a =
similar message, but
           with 256 replaced by 512. It wouldn't be so bad if this only hap=
pened once, but
          I simply cannot afford to have a production machine freeze for mi=
nutes at a time.

     Question 3.1: Does anyone know what's happening and what to do?

          I've gotten both the huge Feb and April 4.0b patches, neither dis=
cuss this issue.

        (And I do know how to set the Fast mode console variables...)

(I'm also calling Digital CSC about these problems; the usual test applies:=
 will the mailing list
come through before Digital or not?)

More questions: 4) our supplier is reporting multi-week delays in getting h=
old of any kind of =

                                 DE500 cards;
                                  does anyone know of a quick source, or al=
ternate 100Mb ethernet cards for =

                                 AlphaServers which work under Digital Unix=
 4.0b?
                              5) Are these problems because we have DE500-X=
A cards instead of DE500-AA's?
                              6) Is there firmware to take care of this?
              =


Thanks in advance for any ideas or sharing of related experiences.


