Yes, you are absolutely right.

I tested the same program with Sun C++ 4.2 and gcc 2.7.2 . 
Sun C++ does exactly what the standard says. The reason you are getting
two destructor messages is there are REALLY two constructions here.
One for the thrown object through the supplied constructor, 
the other for the parameter "a" that is created in the catch() block 
through default copy constructor. If you supply your own copy
constructor (and you should, if you have pointers in your class),
you can see the construction of "a". You should optimise here by
taking the reference to the thrown object since the reference to
the thrown object is valid when the call stack is unwound till we
reach a catch block. Change the 
 catch(exp a)  to
 catche(exp& a) // a is reference to the thrown object
and you will see one construction and one destruction.


Things are really strange with gcc 2.7.2  Apparently,
"throw exp(1);" creates one instance of exp through the
supplied constructor and then creates a copy of it, say x, 
through copy constructor. And the catch parameter "a"
is constructed, as expected ,  from x with the copy constructor. 
If we change the parameter to catch as a reference as
catch(exp& a), x is in any case constructed. The supplied
destrucor is  called for neither a nor x. 

I have the following code which results in memory leak 
which means destructor for either a or x  is not invoked.

class exp {
  public:
   exp(char *s) { 
     i = new char [10];  // allocate 10 instead of strlen(s)
     strcpy(i,s);
     cout << "Constructor exp(" << s << ")" << endl;
   }

   exp(const exp& a) { // copy constructor
     i = new char [10]; // allocation
     strcpy(i, a.i);
     cout << "Copy Constructor exp from " << a.i <<  endl;
   }

   ~exp() {
     cout << "Destructor exp(" << i << ")" << endl;
     delete [] i; // de-allocation
   }

   char *i;

};

int main() {
 try {
   cout << "Throwing exceptions" << endl;
   throw exp("hello");
 }
 catch(exp a) {
   cout << "Caught exception" << a.i << endl;  
 }
 
 cout << "Exiting" << endl;

 return 0;
}

I ran it through purify and it says 10 bytes leaked
out of 30 bytes (10* 3 (= # of objects exp("hello"), x and a)
allocated. Can any gcc guru explain this?

I don't have access to DEC but I am skeptic about exception
implementation in general. Both Sun C++ and gcc behaves same
without any leak, if you change the try-catch block to
following.

 try {
   ...
   throw new exp(1);
   ...
 }
 catch (exp* a) {
   ...

   // even if you don't delete a, memory gets de-allocated, how?
 }


