>Digital still isn't taking Unix very seriously...
>
Its not good news. The word out on the street is that DEC Unix is
going to get worse not better.

On the ADVFS front. I have also been told that using ADVFS on the
system slices is bad news. It shouldn't be. One of the main points of
using jounal file systems as opposed to traditional ufs type file
systems, is the speed in which they recover from crashes. This is
vital in high availability systems. If root has to go through one or
two fsck's and usr at least one, then switchover becomes much slower.
For this reason it is much better to have all filesystems as jfs if
you are using high availibility.

On a practical note, when you build an ADVFS and LSM system, script
the build. Then if you ever have to build again, it is automated
through a script and takes a fraction of the time. We do this as a
matter of course on all veritas volume managers, as the large ones can
be highly complex.



