X-Deja-AN: 242030560
Organization: Canterbury Christ Church College, Kent, UK
Newsgroups: comp.unix.osf.osf1


We are having some problem with BIND on Digital Unix 3.2c.

We have a private IP network with its own number scheme, and our
primary name server has one interface on the our IP network, and
one on the Internet.

Our problem has been that whenever our link to the Internet is
unavailable, any IP connections (e.g ftp, telnet) to this server 
from our IP network become very slow - delays of 1-2 minutes usually.

We have spoken to DEC support and they said that DNS is trying
to do a reverse lookup on our private IP numbers using the DNS root
servers. When the Internet link is unavailable, it cannot contact
the root servers, and so it performs retries and eventually gives
up. DEC says that this is no simple way round this because this is
how DNS works, and so they cannot help us unless we pay for consultancy.

We cannot set up DNS entries for our private IP numbers as we have
over 500 of them, so we need some kind of work around.

Is there a solution to this problem which I could easily implement?
Something like disabling reverse lookups on our private IP numbers?

	Rgds.,

		Nick.

