Stephen Cooper wrote:
> Hmmm RAID 5 and advfs conjures recurring nightmares: Never use advfs
> with RAID 5 unless your SWXCR controller has a battery backup, it can
> completely trash a file domain if some one unplugs yout UPS :(

What does AdvFS have to do with it?  Using any sort of write-back cache
technology without battery backup is asking for trouble.  The
alternative is to use write-through (where the acknowledgement is not
sent until the write is done, rather than just after is it received).

Actually, RAID 5 has this problem, for both write-back and write-
through.  Writes in RAID 5 are not atomic; there is a write-calculate-
write cycle, and that provides a window of opportunity (but the window
is larger for write-back than for write-through).

I don't see what AdvFS has to do with it.  The same problem is there for
UFS.  (Not that everyone should be afraid of RAID 5; battery backup and
related approaches do solve the problem.)
