Newsgroups: comp.unix.osf.osf1,comp.sys.dec


Mark Bartelt <mark@cita.utoronto.ca> wrote:
}Someone just called my attention to a really disturbing
}bug in DEC's C compiler.  It seems that if a program is
}compiled with "-O", then evaluation of "expr1 && expr2"
}is botched:  Even when expr1 evaluates to 0, expr2 gets
}evaluated anyway!
}
}The implications of this bug are rather staggering; for
}example, what if expr2 has side-effects?  Gack.
}
}The bug seems to appear only when "-O" is used.  Here's
}a short example program.  It generates a floating-point
}exception when compiled with "-O".  (It shouldn't, since
}the expression to the right of the "&&" should never be
}evaluated.)

    Gack!  Here's the code w/o and with -O:

#    6	  if ( flag && 1/x > y ) x = 0;

     ldl     $1,   16($sp)                 ldl     $1,   48($sp)
     beq     $1,   $32                     ldit    $f10, 1.0000000000000000e+00
     ldit    $f10, 1.0000000000000000e+00  divt    $f10, $f17, $f11
     ldt     $f11, 24($sp)                 ldt     $f12, 64($sp)
     divt    $f10, $f11, $f12              cmptlt  $f12, $f11, $f13
     ldt     $f13, 32($sp)                 cpys    $f17, $f17, $f14
     cmptlt  $f13, $f12, $f14              fcmovne $f13, $f31, $f14
     fbeq    $f14, $32                     stq     $1,   32($sp)
     stt     $f31, 24($sp)                 ldt     $f15, 32($sp)
$32:                                       cpys    $f14, $f14, $f0
                                           fcmoveq $f15, $f17, $f0

   Obviously they are trying to use conditional
   moves to avoid branches - but this is really
   wrong...

John
