From: mark@cita.toronto.edu (Mark Bartelt)
Subject: horrible "cc -O" bug (DU 3.2c)
Date: 1997/05/09
Message-ID: <E9wuF5.8JE@utcc.utoronto.ca>#1/1
X-Deja-AN: 240575948
Sender: nntp@utcc.utoronto.ca (News)
X-Nntp-Posting-Host: rorqual.cita.utoronto.ca
Organization: Canadian Institute for Theoretical Astrophysics
Reply-To: mark@cita.utoronto.ca
Newsgroups: comp.unix.osf.osf1,comp.sys.dec


Someone just called my attention to a really disturbing
bug in DEC's C compiler.  It seems that if a program is
compiled with "-O", then evaluation of "expr1 && expr2"
is botched:  Even when expr1 evaluates to 0, expr2 gets
evaluated anyway!

The implications of this bug are rather staggering; for
example, what if expr2 has side-effects?  Gack.

The bug seems to appear only when "-O" is used.  Here's
a short example program.  It generates a floating-point
exception when compiled with "-O".  (It shouldn't, since
the expression to the right of the "&&" should never be
evaluated.)

We're running version 3.2c of Digital UNIX.  Can anyone
tell me whether this bug got fixed in 4.0?

-----------------------------------------------------------

moose> cat xx.c
#include <stdio.h>

static double test(int flag, double x, double y)
{
  printf("flag: %d x: %.4g y: %.4g\n", flag, x, y);
  if ( flag && 1/x > y ) x = 0;
  return(x);
}

int main() { (void)test(0, 0.0, 1.0); }
moose>
moose> cc -o xx xx.c
moose> xx
flag: 0 x: 0 y: 1
moose>
moose> cc -O -o xx xx.c
moose> xx
flag: 0 x: 0 y: 1
Floating exception - core dumped
moose>

-----------------------------------------------------------

