In article <roy-0705971511120001@mchip8.med.nyu.edu>, roy@popmail.med.nyu.edu (Roy Smith) writes:

>We just got a couple of alphas running DU-4.0B.  The new AdvFS and LSM
>stuff looks pretty neat, but I'm not sure I've got it all straight.  We're
>going to be running oracle on this system, and it seems like there are a
>variety of ways to manage the storage space of several physical disks.
>
>I could combine all the disks into a single logical volume using LSM.  I
>could then build either a UFS or an AdvFS file system on this logical
>volume and use it for my oracle tablespace.
>
> [ other options deleted... ]

You have a good grasp of the options.  But, you've over simplied
the options a little by skipping over specific features; Mirroring,
striping and volume concatenation.

You could simply glue all the disks together into one big volume
and use it for raw space or a big file system (using device
concatenation).  But then your I/O wouldn't necessarily spread
evenly among the disks.  And if you happen to lose one disk
then you basically lose the whole volume.

Unless you have an array controller, the only way to get mirroring
to use LSM (*).  To LSM's disadvantage it does mirroring at the top
instead of bottom of the device stack, so you have to stripe or
concatenate half your devices together and mirror them to the other
half.  Annoying at times but better than not having mirroring at
all.

So, if you want mirroring use LSM.

AdvFS supports FILE striping.  To use it you create an empty file
and then use the stripe command to tell AdvFS how many physical
volumes you want it spread over.  As the file is fill, AdvFS takes
care of the data distribution.  If you only have a few files you
need striped and can pre-create them, this works fine.

If you want all files on a volume striped, then you want to use
LSM again.  Or, if you want your Oracle database on one big raw
device and want the benefits of striping.

If you don't need either feature and want your data on top of
a single big file system, then ignore LSM and just AdvFS to
create a big domain.  If you have the utilities and find you'll
benefit from striping selected files you can use that later.

