Brian,

The reason for this is that with ULTRIX, Digital shipped a highly-
customized lpd with various command line options.  Not all of these
options for all printers were carried over to Digital UNIX.  

You may have noticed also that the client software to print to the
PrintServer was bundled with ULTRIX but not with Digital UNIX.  A
decision was made long, long ago (back in OSF AXP version 1, in fact)
not to ship this client as part of the operating system any longer
which compounded the problem.

In order to override the default duplex setting on your printer, your
users will need to use the "lpspr" utility provided with the PrintServer 
Software for Digital UNIX.  There are man pages for it (it provides
access to many formatting options, such as number_up, duplex, tray
selection, etc) that your users will likely find useful.  A printable
user-features card is also provided in the /usr/opt/LPS/doc directory,
and you should consider passing them out or posting them someplace
prominent.

To get single sided printing, the command would be:
	
	lpspr -K1 <filename> | lpr -P<printername>

Yes, this is a little awkward for your users.  Another option for you
is to write a wrapper-script for lpr so that your users don't have to
do this explicitly.

Hope this helps.

Sue Gleeson
Former Digital Printing Systems Business engineer 

