We just got a couple of alphas running DU-4.0B.  The new AdvFS and LSM
stuff looks pretty neat, but I'm not sure I've got it all straight.  We're
going to be running oracle on this system, and it seems like there are a
variety of ways to manage the storage space of several physical disks.

I could combine all the disks into a single logical volume using LSM.  I
could then build either a UFS or an AdvFS file system on this logical
volume and use it for my oracle tablespace.

Or, I could just use the logical volume without a file system as a raw
partition for my oracle tablespace.

Or, I could forget about the LSM, and just build a single AdvFS domain
consisting of several volumes, one for each disk, and use this file system
for my oracle tablespace.

Or, I could split my table space up into several datafiles, split between
several UFS file systems, or AdvFS domains, or LMF logical volumes.

Or...

Anyway, you can see my dilema.  As far as I can tell, any of the above
would work, but I really don't have much of a grasp on the advantages and
disadvantages of the various ways of doing things.

Life was so much easier when there was only one way of doing things.  It
may not have been the best way, but at least you didn't have to worry
about making the wrong decision :-)

