In <33703713.41C6@stat.physik.uni-marburg.de> Gerald Ristow wrote:
> Hello,
>    we just bought a DEC-Alpha Workstation 255 at 233 MHz running OSF
> (uname -a gives: OSF1 V4.0 464 alpha). The X-Server should have support
> for OpenGL since the output from xdpyinfo shows the entries:
> number of extensions:    23
>     DEC-PEER-GLX
>     GLX
> or am I mistaken? I also have the GL-include files and some sample
> programs preconfigured on my system. But whenever I run an
> OpenGL-application the system spends some time "thinking" and then the
> X-Server reboots and comes up with the login-window (XDM). This also
> happened with the vrweb program compiled for OpenGL. What do I have to
> do in order to use the OpenGL features? We have the standard PCI
> graphics card 9FX Vision 330 which iI think has a Trio64 chip.

OpenGL isn't supported on the low-end vga cards.  Run the OpenGL
equivalent of 'xdpyinfo' for verification (xglinfo, glxinfo,???, sorry, I
don't have the correct man pages in front of me).  If you had an Open3D
PAK you could, in theory, run the GLonDDX but 1) it's not supported
officially and 2) it would be really, really, really slow.

Why this crashed the server is interesting, however.  If using CDE, check
/var/dt/Xerrors for interesting messages and please file a bug report.

Sometime in the future, a cheap PC 3D card may have accellerated
support.  This is not a promise, blah, blah, blah...

