From: Jean-Claude Magras <magrasjc@texas.net>
Subject: Offering Help with sockets, messages and other IPC
Date: 1997/05/08
Message-ID: <33718FDA.2EF9@texas.net>#1/1
X-Deja-AN: 240164504
Organization: Texas Networking, Inc.
Newsgroups: comp.unix.solaris,comp.unix.programming,comp.unix.osf.osf1


Greetings fellow UNIX programmers,

I am working on a C++ class regarding IPC and devised the appended 
table. I found the attach HTML page which I crated to be useful. You may
want to take out the border=2 clause for compatibility with your
browser. I retain copyright but you may change/redistribute it freely
as long as the copyright is maintained.

Enjoy,

J.C. Magras

<html>
<title>
<h2> Semantic differences between IPC methods
</h2></title>

<table border=2>
<body>
<th> Name
</th>
<th>Primary Descriptor</th>
<th>close uses Primary Descriptor?</th>
<th>fixed ends?</th>
<th>blocks i/o?</th>
<th>data handle vs close descriptor</th>
<tr>
<td>Unix Domain</td>
<td>integer </td>
<td>  yes    </td>
<td>yes</td>
<td>yes</td>
<td>seperate</td>
<tr>
<td>Files</td>
<td>ofstream+ifstream</td>
<td>yes</td>
<td>yes</td>
<td>yes</td>
<td>seperate</td>
<tr>
<td>Internet Sockets</td>
<td>integer </td>
<td>  yes   </td>
<td>  yes   </td>
<td>  yes   </td>
<td>seperate</td>
<tr>
<td>Shared Memory</td>
<td>integer </td>
<td>two step:integer+void*</td>
<td>no-unstructured</td>
<td>no</td>
<td>*SAME*</td>
<tr>
<td>Mapped File</td>
<td>integer+void*</td>
<td>no--void*</td>
<td>no-unstructured</td>
<td>no</td>
<td>*SAME*</td>
<tr>
<td>Message Queue</td>
<td>integer </td>
<td>yes</td>
<td>no-sends to last user</td>
<td>yes</td>
<td>seperate</td>
<tr>
<td>Semaphores</td>
<td>integer</td>
<td>yes</td>
<td>multicast</td>
<td>yes</td>
<td>seperate</td>
<tr>
</table>
<p>
<textblock>
Only counting semaphores hold any user data and vary little at that.<nl>
Semaphores are mostly just signals. Shared Memory/Mapped Files offer<nl>
no data pool access serialization whatsoever. All but sockets need<nl>
file type access rights which are controlled by ioctl(files) or
semctl,shmctl or msgctl.<nl>
The mapped file must be opened with open() (created if necessary)
and<nl> retains its contents even after reboot (as do Unix Domain
sockets).. a security<nl> hazard. The descriptor is then closed and
further manipulations are thru the<nl> address of its own data pool
pointer. Shared memory segments need to<nl> call shmat() and shmdt().
<nl>
</p></textblock>
<b><nl>SEE ALSO</b>
<textblock><p>
mmap(2),munmap(2),shmat(2),shmdt(2),shmget(2),semget(2),msgget(2),semop(2),bind(3N),<nl>
socket(3N),accept(3N),getsockname(3N),select(3C),open(2),close(2),truss(1)<nl>
</p></textblock>
<b><nl>NOTES</b>
<textblock><p>
truss is an excellent system call loging device for catching ipc
errors<nl>
unfortunately, it is only available on Solaris.<nl>
</p></textblock>
