From: stuart@nsl-too.pa.dec.com (Stephen Stuart)
Subject: Re: Volume management?
Date: 1997/05/01
Message-ID: <5k8sc1$bus@usenet.pa.dec.com>#1/1
X-Deja-AN: 238542171
References: <Pine.LNX.3.96.970425153943.17213P-100000@reflections.eng.mindspring.net> <5jtuor$n4g@usenet.pa.dec.com> <33677B76.41C6@lawrence.edu>
Organization: Digital Equipment Corporation, Palo Alto, CA, USA
Newsgroups: comp.unix.osf.osf1


Robert Lowe (Robert.H.Lowe@lawrence.edu) wrote:

: I'll be very interested in doing similar things soon, but I have a
: couple of questions.  If you have a multi-volume advfs domain, and one
: volume fails, the entire domain is unusable, correct?  Then it seems
: wisest to combine advfs with some form of RAID, e.g. hardware or
: software (is this what LSM does?).  I'm not [yet] completely familiar
: with these setups, but adding to a RAID set still means that you would
: have to backup your data, format the bigger RAID set, and restore your
: data, right?  So how does one easily add storage to an advfs domain and
: retain data redundancy?

For critical systems we use advfs on top of RAID; our RAID systems
allow new ranks of disks to be added and formatted on the fly, and
then advfs allows that rank to be added to a file domain on the
fly. We use RAID level 5, so the file domain is usable in the event of
a single disk failure.

gatekeeper.dec.com's archive, for instance, is handled this way:

% showfdmn -k aleph

               Id              Date Created  LogPgs  Domain Name
2ecd7d1f.0006e3f4  Fri Nov 18 20:20:47 1994     512  aleph

  Vol    1K-Blks        Free  % Used  Cmode  Rblks  Wblks  Vol Name 
   1    20939950     8901136     57%     on    128    128  /dev/rza48c
   2    20939950     8949448     57%     on    128    128  /dev/rzb48c
   6L   20938700     8957448     57%     on    128    128  /dev/rza49c
   7    20939950     8970920     57%     on    128    128  /dev/rzb49c
      ----------  ----------  ------
        83758550    35778952     57%

Each volume is a RAID level 5 rank of RZ29B (4GB) disks, for six disks
we get five worth of space; two ranks are on one RAID controller, two
on another.

It is true that the loss of a volume makes an advfs file domain
unusable; there's still no substitute for good backups, and RAID is
still the right choice for high availability. Both of those facts are
true independent of whether you choose UFS or advfs. We choose advfs
just about everywhere - news spools being the one exception.

Stephen
