Message-ID: <33689B9C.41C6@cornell.edu>#1/1
X-Deja-AN: 238648819
Sender: dms35@cornell.edu (Verified)
Organization: Cornell University
Newsgroups: comp.unix.osf.osf1


I have been trying to put a dual tape drive subsystem, incorporating
two
8-mm Exabyte drives and a Peripheral Visions "VisionTray" SCSI bridge,
on
to an AlphaStation 500/333. At the console prompt, the Alpha sometimes
shows
the tape subsytem present on the SCSI bus and sometimes doesn't. When
the
tape system does appear, it has the correct SCSI ID, so it is not in
conflict
with another device. Whether the computer sees the system or not, it
will not
boot. Although we are running DU 4.0B, it appears to start a Windows
boot -
at least there are messages about BIOS starting - this is aborted and
the
power-up countdown appears, followed by the console prompt. There
appears to
be an incompatibility somewhere. Does anyone have any information on
such a
problem and how to correct it?
  The tape subsystem works fine on another AlphaStation here, which
(unfortunately for troubleshooting) differs in several respects from the
500/333.

  System information:

     SCSI bridge: Peripheral Visions VisionTray.
       It displays its firmware level as:  PG-2 Rev126d

     Computer where the problem occurs:
       DEC AlphaStation 500/333,
       operating system Digital UNIX 4.0B,
       console V6.4-5,
       ISP1020 SCSI controller.

     Computer on which there is no problem:
       DEC AlphaStation 600 5/266,
       operating system Digital UNIX 4.0,
       console V6.0-21,
       NCR 810 SCSI controller.

Thanks much,

   Marian Szebenyi
   MacCHESS, Cornell University
