Robert Lowe (Robert.H.Lowe@lawrence.no.spam.edu) wrote:

: [...] I
: don't have a description of the PAF utilities 'balance' command.  Can it
: spread the load across more than just two volumes?  It looked like it
: could from the example you showed.  Once you do that, does it
: automagically retain that balance?  If so, hats off!!!

Yes, balance works across as many volumes as you have. The balance is
not retained; normal use will throw the file domain out of balance,
and cause it to become fragmented. We run defragment and balance out
of crontab, at intervals ranging from nightly to weekly, to maintain
our advfs filesystems.

: Also, when would it make sense to have multiple filesets within a
: domain, since it would seem to result in some contention, or are there
: some on-disk structures that map which extents belong to which fileset,
: and writes are simply spread out in the most efficient way?

You'd have to either consult the documentation to be sure. We think of
a file domain as a container for filesets that share the same
properties. We always separate /var from /usr, so the "/usr" file
domain is shared by filesets that are relatively read-only, while the
"/var" file domain is shared by filesets that are more volatile. 

We defragment and balance nightly on most hosts; the file domain that
holds /usr never takes very long (because its relatively unchanged
since the previous).

